<p>If you believe your claim was handled unfairly, you can ask your insurer for a re-inspection, use the appraisal clause if your policy has one, consult a licensed public adjuster or attorney, or contact the Texas Department of Insurance.</p>

<h2>Deductibles: The Rule Every Texas Homeowner Should Know</h2>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><p><strong>Be wary of any roofer who offers to “cover” or “waive” your deductible.</strong> Under Chapter 707 of the Texas Insurance Code, it is illegal for a contractor to knowingly waive, absorb or rebate all or part of an insured’s deductible — including through fake discounts, “advertising” payments or inflated invoices. Austin Roofing does not do this, and you should be cautious of anyone who does.</p></div>
<p>Many Texas homeowners policies use a separate wind and hail deductible, often written as a percentage of your dwelling coverage rather than a flat dollar amount. On a home insured for $400,000, a 2% wind/hail deductible would be $8,000. Check your declarations page now, before a storm, so you know what to expect.</p>

<h2>Filing an Insurance Claim for Roof Hail Damage in Austin: Step by Step</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><h3>Stay safe and prevent further damage</h3><p>Contain leaks, move belongings and arrange emergency tarping if the roof is open. Keep receipts — your policy likely requires reasonable steps to limit damage.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Get a free inspection</h3><p>Before you file, it helps to know whether there is real storm damage. We inspect and tell you honestly if we do not see enough damage to justify a claim.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Read your policy</h3><p>Look at your deductible, whether roof coverage is replacement cost or actual cash value, any roof age schedule or cosmetic damage exclusion, and the deadline for reporting damage.</p></li>
  <li><h3>File the claim with your insurer</h3><p>Report the date of the storm and the damage. You file the claim yourself, directly with your insurance company or agent.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Adjuster inspection</h3><p>Your insurer schedules an adjuster. Let us know the date and we will meet them on the roof with our photo report.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Review the estimate</h3><p>When the insurer’s estimate arrives, we can walk you through the roofing line items and point out any physical damage or code-required items we believe were missed, so you can raise them with your insurer.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Repairs and final documentation</h3><p>Once you decide to proceed, we complete the work and provide invoices and completion photos — often needed to release recoverable depreciation on replacement-cost policies.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2>ACV vs Replacement Cost: Why It Matters</h2>
<p>With a <strong>replacement cost value (RCV)</strong> policy, the insurer typically pays the actual cash value first, then releases the withheld depreciation once the work is complete. With an <strong>actual cash value (ACV)</strong> policy, the payout is reduced by the roof’s depreciation and that money is not recovered — so an older roof may pay out far less than the cost of a new one. Some Texas policies apply ACV only to roofs, or use a roof age payment schedule. Our guide to <a href="/blog/insurance-coverage-for-storm-damaged-roofs/">insurance coverage for storm-damaged roofs</a> covers these differences in more detail.</p>

<h2>Austin-Specific Tips for Storm Claims</h2>
<p>Central Texas hail season peaks in spring, and after a big storm, adjusters, roofers and out-of-town crews all get stretched thin. A few habits help:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Write down the date and time of every hailstorm or major windstorm that hits your home.</li>
  <li>Photograph hail on the ground next to a coin or ruler, and any dents on cars, gutters or AC units.</li>
  <li>Be cautious of door-knockers who appear after a storm with no local presence, ask you to sign a contract on the spot, or offer to handle your claim.</li>
  <li>Keep a copy of your roof’s installation date and any past inspection reports — they help counter “old damage” arguments.</li>
  <li>Use our <a href="/roofing-tools/hail-damage-checker/">hail damage checker</a> to see what the hail size you saw typically does to your roof type.</li>
</ul>

